[Event "Rook endgames: behind the passed pawn"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/vZQa2yT6"]
[FEN "8/8/1k1P4/1r6/R5K1/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/1k1P4/1r6/R5K1/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{Rook endings: everybody reaches them, almost nobody knows them. First rule — your rook belongs behind the passed pawn. [%cal Ya4d4]}
1. Rd4 {Rd4 tucks your rook in behind the pawn, exactly where it belongs. Nothing on the board can stop it now. [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd6d8]} (1. d7 {Running the pawn to d7 alone is the mistake: with no rook behind it, his rook simply blocks it in front, on d5.} 1... Rd5 2. d8=Q+ Rxd8 {Your new queen lasts one move: his rook takes her straight back on d8. Drawn.}) (1. Ra6+ {Ra6+ is a free check, but it hangs your rook for nothing: he simply takes it.} 1... Kxa6 {The rook is gone, taken for nothing on a6, and the pawn falls next. Lost.}) (1. Rb4 {Offering the trade with Rb4 delights him: he takes it at once, and your pawn stands alone.} 1... Rxb4+ {Now the pawn on d6 has nobody left to guard it, and it falls. You are lost.}) (1. Ra5 {Ra5 goes sideways and lands your rook right beside his, where it simply gets taken.} 1... Rxa5 {The rook is gone, traded for nothing on a5, and the pawn is left defenceless. You are lost.}) (1. Ra8 {Ra8 is far from the pawn and far from safe: it does nothing useful sitting on the back row.} 1... Rb4+ 2. Kf3 Rb3+ {His checks never stop from there, and your king can never escape them. Drawn.}) 1... Kc5 {He comes after your rook on d4. Push the pawn on to d7 regardless of the threat.} 2. d7 {Your rook shields the pawn from behind, and it queens no matter what he tries. Now watch it go home. [%csl Gd7][%tail]} (2. Rd1 {Rd1 also wins: it is still behind the pawn, just watching from further back down the column.} 2... Kc6 {The pawn walks home just the same from there, with the rook still shielding it from checks.}) (2. Rd2 {Rd2 is the same idea, one square along the column, and it wins exactly the same way.} 2... Kc6 {Behind the pawn is behind the pawn, wherever exactly you stand. Still winning.}) (2. Rd3 {Rd3 is also fine: any square directly behind the pawn on the d column does the job.} 2... Kc6 {The pawn walks home from here too, since the rook behind it never lets a check through.}) (2. Rd5+ {Rd5+ is a check, but his king simply captures the rook you have just handed him.} 2... Kxd5 3. d7 Rb8 {His other rook swings round to b8 and blocks the pawn's road for good. You are lost.}) (2. Rc4+ {Rc4+ hangs the rook the same way: another check that costs you the piece outright.} 2... Kxc4 3. Kf3 Rd5 {The pawn on d6 falls next with no rook left to defend it. You are lost.}) (2. Ra4 {Ra4 abandons the pawn completely on d6, leaving it to fend for itself against his king.} 2... Kxd6 {His king simply walks up and eats the undefended pawn on d6. Drawn.}) 2... Kxd4 3. d8=Q+ {A new queen appears on d8, with check. A queen against a lone rook is a straightforward win. [%csl Gd8]} 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: get behind his runner"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/5haHjaqz"]
[FEN "4k3/8/3K4/8/4P3/8/7r/R7 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/8/3K4/8/4P3/8/7r/R7_w_-_-_0_1"]

{He is a pawn up, and his king already sits on d6, ready to escort that pawn home. A draw is a fine result here: can you hold it?}
1. e5 {The pawn sets off, pushing to e5. Which square kills it stone dead in its tracks?} 1... Re2 {Re2 stands right behind it on the e column, and now it cannot take another single step.} (1... Rd2+ {Rd2+ only helps him: the check drives his king exactly where it wants to go.} 2. Ke6 Kd8 3. Ra8+ {You are driven back off the d column, and the pawn walks straight in behind you. Lost.}) (1... Rh6+ {Rh6+ has the same trouble: the check sends his king right where it wants to be.} 2. e6 Kf8 3. Ra8+ {You are lost. His rook pins yours to the back row while the pawn queens.}) (1... Kf7 {Stepping aside with Kf7 sets the pawn free to run all the way to e8 unopposed.} 2. e6+ Kf6 3. Rf1+ {Lost. The pawn runs while your king chases checks instead of the runner.}) (1... Ra2 {Offering the trade with Ra2 is one you cannot afford: without rooks, his pawn simply runs.} 2. Rxa2 {A bare king against a pawn and a king now, with nothing left to stop it. Lost.}) (1... Rh1 {Rh1 wanders off into the corner, and the rook is not even safe once it gets there.} 2. Rxh1 {Lost. Your rook is gone for nothing, and the pawn walks in right behind it.}) 2. e6 {He pushes past your rook, to e6. Now which check does your rook have left to give?} 2... Rd2+ {Check from the d column, and his king has to leave d6. Your king on e8 already sits in the pawn's road, so now watch his try fail. [%csl Gd2][%cal Ge8e6][%tail]} (2... Rxe6+ {Taking the free pawn with Rxe6+ looks great, but look at what is left on the board after it.} 3. Kxe6 {A bare king against his king and rook now, with nothing to hold them off. You are lost.}) (2... Re1 {Re1 gets behind the pawn again, but his king was already quicker to the corner.} 3. Ra8# {Mate! Your king is boxed in on the back row with no square left to run to.}) (2... Kf8 {Kf8 wanders your king off the pawn's own file, right when it needed to stay there.} 3. Ra8+ {And the pawn queens right behind you on e8, with your king facing the wrong way. Lost.}) 3. Ke5 3... Ke7 4. Ra7+ 4... Ke8 5. e7 5... Rd7 {You offer the trade on d7. With the rooks gone, nobody is left to walk that pawn home.} 6. Rxd7 6... Kxd7 7. e8=Q+ 7... Kxe8 {A new queen appears on e8, and your king eats her at once. Two bare kings on an empty board: a draw. [%csl Ge8]} 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: your rook is attacked"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/Gi4bWJcb"]
[FEN "8/4k3/8/8/1r6/K3p3/6R1/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/4k3/8/8/1r6/K3p3/6R1/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{His king is attacking your rook, and your own pawn needs a friend. One square does both jobs at once.}
1... Re4 {Re4 tucks in behind your own pawn, out of his king's reach. Now the pawn can never be blocked from the front. [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge3e1]} (1... e2 {Racing the pawn to e2 alone runs it straight into his rook, with nothing there to help it.} 2. Rxe2+ Kd7 3. Kxb4 {Your pawn on e2 is gone, and now your rook falls to his king too. Lost.}) (1... Ra4+ {Ra4+ is a check, but the rook lands right where his king can simply take it.} 2. Kxa4 {The rook is gone, and the game goes with it, since the pawn has nobody left. Lost.}) (1... Rb3+ {The same idea as Ra4+, and the same problem: another check with a hanging rook.} 2. Kxb3 {Lost. His king simply steps up and captures the rook that just checked it.}) (1... Rb2 {Rb2 guards the pawn from the side, but it stands right next to his own king.} 2. Kxb2 {Lost. The rook stood beside his king instead of behind your pawn, and now it is simply gone.}) (1... Kd6 {Kd6 brings the king over to help, but it leaves the rook on b4 hanging for free.} 2. Kxb4 {Lost. His king scoops up the unguarded rook on b4, and the pawn falls next.}) 2. Re2 {He blocks right in front of the pawn on e2. It cannot move by itself now, so who can move it forward?} 2... Kd6 {Your king marches over to help. Nothing else on the board can push that pawn through. [%csl Gd6]} (2... Ke6 {Ke6 is also good: any step toward the pawn on e3 does the exact same job.} 3. Kb2 Ke5 {Still winning: your king arrives at the pawn on e3 just the same, one way or another.}) (2... Kf6 {Kf6 is also fine, just one square slower to reach the pawn on e3.} 3. Kb2 Ke5 {Still winning: your king reaches the pawn on e3 just as well starting from here.}) 3. Kb2 {His king runs back toward e3 to help defend it. Keep your own king marching in.} 3... Ke5 {Straight at the pawn. Your king gets there first, and nothing stops it now. Now watch it march the pawn home. [%csl Ge5][%tail]} (3... Kd5 {Kd5 also heads toward the pawn, but it is one row too low to actually help.} 4. Kc3 {His king slips in front of the pawn on e3, and the win you had is gone.}) (3... Rd4 {Rd4 wanders the rook away and leaves your pawn with no guard at all.} 4. Rxe3 {The pawn on e3 is simply eaten by his rook, with nobody left to defend it. Drawn.}) 4. Kc1 4... Kf4 5. Re1 5... Kf3 6. Rh1 6... Rc4+ 7. Kd1 7... e2+ 8. Kd2 8... Rd4+ 9. Kc2 9... Kg2 10. Rh4 10... Rxh4 {You take the rook on h4. His king stands alone against a king, a rook, and a pawn one step from queening.} 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: build the wall"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/fdGo4RY7"]
[FEN "8/k7/8/3P4/r7/3K4/6R1/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/k7/8/3P4/r7/3K4/6R1/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{A new word: cutting off. Build a wall with your rook, and his king can never cross to the other side of it. [%cal Yg2b2]}
1. Rb2 {Rb2 puts the wall up on the b column. His king is locked out on the wrong side of it for good. [%csl Gb2][%cal Gb2b8]} (1. d6 {Running the pawn to d6 too soon starts the checks, and there is no wall left to shelter your king from them.} 1... Ra3+ 2. Ke2 Ra2+ {His checks never stop from there, and your king can never find safety. Drawn.}) (1. Rg7+ {Rg7+ is a check that chases his king nowhere useful, and it costs you the wall.} 1... Ka6 2. d6 Ra1 {He slips his rook behind the pawn on d6 and holds the position firmly. Drawn.}) (1. Ra2 {Offering the trade with Ra2 is one he is only too happy to take off the board.} 1... Rxa2 {His rook is left standing on the board, and yours is not. You are lost.}) (1. Rg4 {Rg4 steps the rook into the open, right where his rook can take it.} 1... Rxg4 {You are lost. His rook simply takes yours on g4 for nothing in return.}) (1. Kc3 {Walking the king up with Kc3 lets the checks arrive first, before your king is ready for them.} 1... Ra3+ 2. Kb2 Rd3 {He gets in front of the pawn on d6 before your king can help it. Drawn.}) 1... Rf4 {He shuffles his rook about behind the wall. Now walk your king up to join it. [%cal Yd3e3]} 2. Ke3 {Your king climbs, the wall still holds, and the pawn follows behind it. Now watch it walk home. [%csl Ge3][%tail]} (2. Kc3 {Kc3 also wins: your king can climb up either side of the wall on the b column.} 2... Rf3+ 3. Kc4 Rf4+ {Still winning: his checks slow you down, but they never catch your king.}) (2. Rb1 {Rb1 is also fine: the wall is a whole column wide, not one fixed square on it.} 2... Rf3+ {His checks keep coming from behind, but the wall on the b column still holds firm.}) (2. Rb5 {Rb5 also wins: the wall can slide to any square it likes up the b column.} 2... Ka6 {Still winning: the wall holds no matter which exact square on the column it sits on today.}) (2. d6 {Pushing to d6 too soon here needs the king's support first, not a head start alone.} 2... Rf6 3. d7 Rd6+ {He wins the pawn back on d6, and the position settles into a draw.}) (2. Rb7+ {Rb7+ is a check that simply gives the rook away to his own king.} 2... Kxb7 {You are lost. His king simply steps up and captures the rook that just checked it.}) (2. Ra2+ {Ra2+ trades the wall for one single check, and that is not a fair price.} 2... Kb6 3. d6 Rf1 {He is back in the game with his rook active again, and the position holds. Drawn.}) 2... Rc4 3. d6 3... Ka6 4. d7 4... Rc7 5. d8=Q {A new queen stands on d8. The wall never once let his king or his rook anywhere near the action.} 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: a wall on the fifth rank"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "review"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/2jeUUw1L"]
[FEN "4r3/1R6/8/4P3/8/8/k5K1/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4r3/1R6/8/4P3/8/8/k5K1/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{His king is miles away in the corner. Build a wall with your rook and keep him there.}
1. Rb5 {Rb5 puts a wall along the fifth row. His king can never climb above it. [%csl Gb5][%cal Ga5h5]} (1. e6 {Running the pawn to e6 alone sends it straight where his rook is already waiting.} 1... Rxe6 {The pawn is gone, eaten on e6. Drawn. It ran off with no king there to guard it.}) (1. Ra7+ {Ra7+ is a check, but once it is over the pawn on e5 still falls anyway.} 1... Kb3 2. e6 Rxe6 {Drawn. The check bought no time at all, and his rook simply eats the runner.}) (1. Kf3 {Bringing the king up with Kf3 leaves the pawn on e5 hanging with no rook behind it.} 1... Rxe5 {Drawn. Undefended, the pawn on e5 falls to his rook in a single move.}) (1. Rb2+ {Rb2+ steps the rook right next to his king, where it simply gets taken.} 1... Kxb2 {You are lost. His king simply captures the rook that just checked it.}) (1. Rb4 {Rb4 is one row short of the fifth, and the wall leaks a whole rank through it.} 1... Rxe5 {The pawn is gone. Drawn. His rook slips through the gap and takes it.}) 1... Ka3 {He wanders near his own corner on a3, going nowhere useful at all.} 2. Kf3 {Your king sets off, and the wall on the fifth row does the guarding by itself. [%csl Gf3]} (2. Kg3 {Kg3 also wins: any road toward the pawn is fine while the wall holds.} 2... Ka4 3. Rd5 {Still winning: the wall just slides to a new square on the same row, and it still reaches from a5 to h5. [%cal Ga5h5]}) (2. Rd5 {Rd5 is also good, and you could just as well play this move a little later.} 2... Ka4 3. Kf3 {Still winning: your king strolls up while the wall guards the pawn all by itself.}) (2. Kf2 {Kf2 also wins: any road your king takes toward the pawn on e5 will do.} 2... Ka4 {Still winning: whichever path your king takes, it gets there just the same.}) (2. e6 {e6 still cannot run alone: the pawn needs an escort before it moves.} 2... Rxe6 {Drawn. It had no escort at all, and his rook simply eats it on e6.}) (2. Ra5+ {Ra5+ is a check that throws away the wall you had just spent moves building.} 2... Kb4 3. Ra1 Rxe5 {Drawn. Chasing the check let go of the wall, and the pawn falls.}) (2. Rb3+ {Rb3+ puts the rook just one step from his king, right where it gets taken.} 2... Kxb3 {You are lost. The check landed right next to his king, and it simply takes the rook.}) 2... Ka4 {He attacks your rook. Save it, but keep the wall standing.} 3. Rd5 {The rook is safe, the wall still holds, and now it guards the pawn too. Now watch it march home. [%csl Gd5][%tail]} (3. Rc5 {Rc5 is also good: the wall just needs to stay somewhere on the fifth row.} 3... Ka3 {Still winning: his king wanders about while the wall keeps doing its job.}) (3. e6 {Pushing with e6 here forgets that your rook on b5 was just attacked and left hanging.} 3... Kxb5 {The rook is gone. You are lost. His king simply eats the rook you left hanging.}) (3. Rb4+ {Rb4+ hands over the whole rook in exchange for just one single check.} 3... Kxb4 {Lost. You traded the rook for one check, and nothing is left to escort the pawn.}) 3... Kb3 4. Ke4 4... Rxe5+ 5. Rxe5 {You take back on e5. His rook is gone, and a bare king can never trouble a king and rook.} 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: the defender builds one too"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/QrceVMS9"]
[FEN "4R3/8/8/rp6/4P3/2K5/8/1k6 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4R3/8/8/rp6/4P3/2K5/8/1k6_b_-_-_0_1"]

{His pawn on e4 wants to run, and his king on c3 is heading for your pawn on b5. Your job is to survive: one move stops both.}
1... Ra4 {Ra4 puts a wall on the fourth row. It blocks his pawn and his king at the very same time. [%csl Ga4][%cal Ga4h4]} (1... b4+ {Pushing with check on b4 feels great, but it loses everything you have.} 2. Kxb4 Ra7 3. Kc5 {Your pawn on b5 is gone, and his own pawn on e4 now runs free. Lost.}) (1... Ra3+ {A check with Ra3+ first, and the wall on the fourth row never gets built at all.} 2. Kb4 Ra7 3. e5 {His pawn on e4 gets clean away while you were busy checking. Lost.}) (1... Ra2 {Ra2 is too low a row to block anything: his pawn simply walks straight past it.} 2. e5 Ra4 3. Rb8 {Lost. His rook slips behind your own pawn on b5 and stops it there cold.}) (1... Ra8 {Offering the trade with Ra8 turns this into a lost pawn ending straight away.} 2. Rxa8 b4+ 3. Kb3 {Lost. Without rooks on the board, his extra pawn just walks home.}) (1... Kc1 {Kc1 steps your king out and forgets about the pawn on b5 entirely.} 2. e5 Ra4 3. Rb8 {Lost. His rook gets behind your pawn, and your king never came to help it.}) 2. e5 {His pawn on e4 walks right up to your wall on the fourth row. Now hold your ground.} 2... Re4 {You slide your rook along the wall and stand right in front of him. [%csl Ge4]} (2... b4+ {The same check again, and it costs you everything a second time.} 3. Kb3 Ra2 4. e6 {His pawn on e4 queens first, and there is nothing left to stop it. Lost.}) (2... Rc4+ {Rc4+ is a check, but it gives up the wall on the fourth row you were holding.} 3. Kd3 Rc1 4. Rb8 {Lost. His rook gets behind your pawn now that yours has left the row.}) (2... Rb4 {Rb4 guards your own pawn on b5 instead of standing in his king's way.} 3. Kxb4 {The rook is gone, taken by his king with no support nearby. Lost.}) (2... Ra3+ {Ra3+ steps your rook off the fourth row, and his pawn on e4 runs free.} 3. Kb4 Re3 4. Ka5 {Lost. His king steps clear of the checks, and the pawn queens next.}) 3. e6 {He pushes past you, to e6. Now your own pawn is the one that saves you.} 3... b4+ {Now the check works: it drives his king away from everything at once. Now watch your try play out. [%csl Gb4][%tail]} (3... Rxe6 {Grabbing the free pawn with Rxe6 lets his rook simply take yours right back.} 4. Rxe6 {Your rook is gone, and his pawn on e4 queens with nothing left to stop it. Lost.}) (3... Re5 {Blocking with Re5 in front of the pawn, instead of checking, is the wrong idea.} 4. Kd4 {His king attacks your rook, and the pawn goes straight through. Lost.}) (3... Rc4+ {That is the wrong check: his king simply steps up and shelters beside the pawn.} 4. Kd3 {Lost. His king shelters right next to the pawn, and the check achieved nothing.}) 4. Kd2 4... Rc4 5. e7 5... Rc7 6. Kd3 6... b3 7. Rb8 7... Rxe7 {Your rook finally takes on e7, because his own rook had to leave e8 to chase your runner on b3.} 8. Rxb3+ 8... Ka1 {Both pawns are gone, and only the rooks are left. Neither king can ever be mated, so the game is a draw.} 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: a busy rook beats a sleepy one"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/GCG0qpGy"]
[FEN "8/8/2P5/2R5/kr6/8/6K1/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/2P5/2R5/kr6/8/6K1/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{You are a pawn down and playing for the draw. New idea: a busy rook. Right now yours sits on b4, next to your king, doing nothing at all. [%cal Yb4b8]}
1... Rb8 {Rb8 leaves the king alone and takes the only job on the board that actually matters. [%csl Gb8]} (1... Rb2+ {Rb2+ checks, and checks again, but none of those checks slow the pawn down at all.} 2. Kf3 Rb3+ 3. Ke4 {The pawn just walks home to c8 with nothing there to trouble it. Lost.}) (1... Rb6 {Attacking the pawn from the side with Rb6 does nothing to actually stop it.} 2. Rc4+ Kb5 3. c7 {It reaches the last row and queens, with your rook still watching from the side. Lost.}) (1... Rb7 {Rb7 sits one row too high, right where the pawn wants to capture it.} 2. cxb7 {Lost. Your rook stood exactly on the square the pawn needed to capture on its way up.}) (1... Rc4 {Offering the trade with Rc4 hands him the whole race: a pawn runs faster with no rook in its way.} 2. Rxc4+ {Lost. Without rooks left on the board, the pawn simply runs home unopposed.}) (1... Kb3 {Kb3 wanders the king while the rook stays asleep on b4, doing nothing at all.} 2. Kf3 Rb8 3. Ke4 {Too slow: his king reaches the pawn before your king can ever help. Lost.}) 2. c7 {The pawn runs for the last row, on c7. Get your rook in front of it now.} 2... Rc8 {Rc8 is the only square in the world that stops it, and it holds the draw right there for good. Now watch him try to break through. [%csl Gc8][%tail]} (2... Rg8+ {Checking first with Rg8+ costs you the one tempo you needed to reach c8 in time.} 3. Kf3 Rc8 4. Ke4 {His king arrives at c8 in time to help the pawn queen. Lost.}) (2... Ra8 {Ra8 has the right row but the wrong column: it does not actually block anything.} 3. Rc4+ Ka5 4. Rc5+ {Lost. Your rook sat behind the pawn instead of in front of it, and his checks find your king instead.}) (2... Rb7 {Rb7 gets behind the pawn, but that is too late once it has reached the seventh row.} 3. Rc4+ {Lost. The check drives your king off the square, and the pawn queens right after.}) (2... Kb4 {Kb4 brings the king over but leaves the rook right where the pawn can eat it.} 3. cxb8=Q+ {A new queen appears on c8, and your rook is captured right along with it. Lost.}) 3. Rc1 3... Kb4 4. Rb1+ 4... Ka4 {You step your king aside. His rook can check all day, but yours never leaves c8 and his pawn never gets to move. Drawn.} 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: check him from far away"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/4sAAyheO"]
[FEN "4k3/1R6/4PK2/8/8/8/8/r7 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/1R6/4PK2/8/8/8/8/r7_b_-_-_0_1"]

{Mate is one move away on the back row, and blocking will not save you. You are down a pawn, so the draw is all you want: find the move.}
1... Rf1+ {Rf1+ checks from the f column, five squares from his king, where he can never chase your rook down. Now watch him try everything. [%csl Gf1][%tail]} (1... Ra7 {Ra7 guards the back row but forgets about the other row entirely.} 2. Rb8# {Mate! Your king is walled in by its own pawns, with no square to answer the check.}) (1... Ra6 {The same idea on a6, and it leads straight to the same mate again.} 2. Rb8# {Mate! The rook delivers it straight down the open b column with nothing to block it.}) (1... Ra8 {Blocking on the back row with Ra8 does nothing: he simply steps his king aside.} 2. Rg7 Kd8 3. Rg8+ {You are lost. The check drives your king off the row, and mate follows.}) (1... Kd8 {The king runs to d8, but his rook comes right along to keep chasing it.} 2. Rb8+ Kc7 3. Rf8 {Lost. His rook cuts yours off from the whole board, and it can never get back in time.}) (1... Rd1 {A check would have saved you here. Blocking with Rd1 stops nothing at all.} 2. Rb8+ Rd8 3. Rxd8+ {Lost. Blocking with the rook cost you the rook itself, as well as the whole game.}) 2. Kg6 2... Rg1+ 3. Kh6 3... Rh1+ {Check again. His king can run all it likes, but it can never trap your rook and free the pawn in the same move. Drawn.} 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: give the pawn, free the rook"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/eDO2sKMh"]
[FEN "3R4/8/7P/8/3p4/7k/r7/1K6 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3R4/8/7P/8/3p4/7k/r7/1K6_b_-_-_0_1"]

{His pawn on h6 is nearly home, and his king on b1 is about to eat your rook on a2. Something has to give: hold the draw.}
1... Ra7 {Ra7 climbs to the seventh row. Your own pawn falls, but that h pawn can never get past your rook now. Now watch him try. [%csl Ga7][%cal Ga7h7][%tail]} (1... d3 {Pushing your own pawn with d3 loses the rook instead of saving it.} 2. Kxa2 Kg4 3. Rxd3 {Both pawns are gone from the board, and his king and rook win easily. Lost.}) (1... Ra1+ {Ra1+ checks right next to his own king, walking straight into its reach.} 2. Kxa1 {Lost. Your rook walked straight into the king it was checking.}) (1... Ra8 {Offering the trade with Ra8 lets the h pawn queen once the rooks are gone.} 2. Rxa8 {Lost. His rook takes yours on a8, and the h pawn runs home completely alone.}) (1... Ra6 {Ra6 is one row short, and he simply pushes the pawn straight past it.} 2. h7 Rb6+ 3. Kc2 {The pawn queens. Lost. The check costs a tempo, and his pawn runs past on the very next move.}) (1... Rd2 {Guarding your own pawn with Rd2 is exactly what you cannot afford here.} 2. Kc1 Rf2 3. h7 {Lost. Your rook is stuck watching a pawn that was never the real danger.}) 2. h7 2... Rxh7 {Your rook simply takes on h7. His last pawn is gone, and the draw is safe.} 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: the Philidor defence"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/iwpoPqvD"]
[FEN "2k5/6R1/7r/1KP5/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2k5/6R1/7r/1KP5/8/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{Meet the Philidor defence, the way you hold a pawn down: your king stays in front of his pawn on c8, and your rook waits on the sixth row until he pushes past it.}
1. c6 {He pushes his pawn past your rook, to c6. That is your signal: drop the rook right down to the first row. [%cal Yh6h1]} 1... Rh1 {Rh1 checks him from the first row forever now, and he can never hide from it. [%csl Gh1][%cal Gh1h5][%tail]} (1... Rh2 {Rh2 is also a draw: any square down on the first row gives the very same endless checks.} 2. Rg8+ Kc7 {Still holding: your rook checks from the first row just the same.}) (1... Rh3 {Rh3 is the same idea, one square along, and it holds just as well.} 2. Rg8+ Kc7 {Drawn. Every square on that row gives the same endless checks.}) (1... Rh4 {Rh4 is also fine: getting down to the first row is what matters, not exactly how far along it.} 2. Rg8+ Kc7 {Drawn. His king can never escape a rook checking along an empty row.}) (1... Rxc6 {Grabbing the free pawn with Rxc6 turns a draw into a lost ending.} 2. Kxc6 Kd8 3. Ra7 {King and rook against your bare king now, with the pawn already gone too. Lost.}) (1... Rh5+ {Rh5+ checks from too close: his king simply walks straight at your rook.} 2. Kb6 Rd5 3. Rg8+ {Lost. Your rook stood too close to check from, and the pawn queens next.}) (1... Kd8 {Kd8 walks your king away from the pawn's own square, on c8, right when it needed to stay.} 2. Kb6 Ke8 3. Kb7 {The pawn queens. Lost. Nothing was left behind to stop it.}) (1... Rh8 {Dropping back with Rh8 has the right idea, but the first row was the target, not the eighth.} 2. Kb6 Kb8 3. Rb7+ {Lost. Your rook sits on the wrong row to give a single useful check.}) (1... Rh7 {Offering the trade with Rh7 is one he is only too happy to take off the board.} 2. Rxh7 {Lost. The rook that was holding the draw by itself is now simply gone.}) 2. Kb6 2... Rb1+ 3. Kc5 3... Rc1+ {Check again, and the pattern never ends: his king can wander all day, but your rook always finds the row it needs. Drawn.} 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: your king belongs in front"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/m3sJI5LK"]
[FEN "4k3/8/6P1/6K1/8/8/3R4/r7 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/8/6P1/6K1/8/8/3R4/r7_b_-_-_0_1"]

{His pawn on g6 has two steps left, and his king on g5 is escorting it home. Saving the draw is all you can hope for: where does your king have to be?}
1... Kf8 {Kf8 stands right in front of the pawn. That single square is the whole defence. [%csl Gf8]} (1... Rg1+ {Checking first with Rg1+ just lets his king step around it and keep escorting the pawn.} 2. Kf6 Rf1+ 3. Kg7 {The pawn queens. Lost. The checks never touched the pawn's own road home.}) (1... Ke7 {Ke7 is one square out, and it is already too late to hold the draw from there.} 2. Kh6 Rh1+ 3. Kg7 {Lost. His king reaches g7 to guard the pawn, and it queens on the very next move.}) (1... Rf1 {Guarding the queening square from the side with Rf1 is not enough on its own.} 2. Kh6 Rh1+ 3. Kg7 {Lost. The check comes a move too late to keep his king off the square on g7.}) (1... Ra5+ {Ra5+ checks him right where his king wanted to go anyway, straight toward the corner.} 2. Kh6 Ra6 3. Kh7 {Lost. His king reaches the corner and shields the pawn all the way home.}) (1... Ra7 {The seventh row does not help against a pawn already on the sixth.} 2. Kh6 Kf8 3. Rd8+ {Lost. The check drives your king away, and the pawn queens right behind it.}) 2. g7+ {He pushes the pawn to g7 with check. Now help yourself to it right away.} 2... Kxg7 {The pawn is gone, and a lone rook can never mate you. Drawn. [%csl Gg7]} (2... Kf7 {Kf7 is also a draw: from there the pawn cannot run anywhere at all.} 3. Rd7+ Kg8 {You will eat the pawn on g7 next move, and the game is a clean draw.}) (2... Kg8 {Kg8 is also fine, and the pawn falls just the same either way.} 3. Rd8+ Kxg7 {Drawn. The check buys the tempo to scoop up the pawn all the same.}) (2... Ke7 {Stepping away from the pawn with Ke7 lets it run without any king to stop it.} 3. Kg6 {His king walks the pawn all the way home to queen. Lost.}) (2... Ke8 {The same mistake as Ke7, just moved one square further along.} 3. g8=Q+ {Lost. A new queen is born with check, and your rook cannot hold a whole queen off.}) 0-1


[Event "Rook endgames: drop back far enough"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "review"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/xQSoo5al"]
[FEN "8/8/8/8/4k3/R2p4/7r/3K4 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/8/4k3/R2p4/7r/3K4_w_-_-_0_1"]

{You are the one defending now, so holding the draw is the job. His pawn is close, and your rook stands far too near it to check safely.}
1. Ra5 {Ra5 drops back far enough to keep checking safely: from there his king can never walk up and swallow the check. [%csl Ga5][%tail]} (1. Ra6 {Ra6 is also a draw: dropping back even further still leaves the rook enough room to check.} 1... d2 2. Ra3 {You still hold the draw from there, just as safely as the first square.}) (1. Ra7 {Ra7 is the same idea again: any of these back squares holds the draw.} 1... d2 2. Ra3 {Drawn. Your rook simply slides straight back into checking range from a3.}) (1. Ra8 {Ra8 goes all the way back into the corner, and it is also perfectly fine.} 1... d2 2. Ra3 {Drawn. Even from the far corner, the rook gets back in time to check.}) (1. Rxd3 {Grabbing the free pawn with Rxd3 costs you the rook outright to his king.} 1... Kxd3 2. Ke1 Ra2 {You are lost. His king shelters on the back row while your rook stands there all alone.}) (1. Ra4+ {Ra4+ checks from right beside him, and he simply steps up past it.} 1... Ke3 2. Rg4 Rh1+ {Lost. Your rook has no more useful checks left, and the pawn queens.}) (1. Ra1 {Ra1 sits too close to the last row now, and his rook comes with check.} 1... Rh1+ 2. Kd2 Rxa1 {Lost. The check wins him the tempo to capture your rook outright.}) (1. Rb3 {Rb3 goes sideways, and it does nothing at all to slow the pawn or shelter your king.} 1... Ke3 2. Kc1 Rh1+ {Lost. Check, and there is no square left on the row to block it from.}) (1. Kc1 {The king runs off with Kc1 and leaves the rook stranded on its own.} 1... Ke3 2. Rb3 Rh1+ {Lost. Check, and your king has nowhere safe left on the board to hide.}) 1... d2 2. Ra3 2... Kd4 3. Ra4+ {Check again, from that same safe distance. His king has no shelter from the checks, and his pawn has no escort. Drawn.} 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: the Lucena bridge"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/DvfV31FU"]
[FEN "8/5P1k/8/r2K4/4R3/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/5P1k/8/r2K4/4R3/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{The famous Lucena position. He is checking you, so walk your king straight at the checks, never away from them. [%cal Yd5e6]}
1. Ke6 {Ke6 walks toward the checking rook, never away from it. That is the whole idea of the climb. [%csl Ge6]} (1. Kd6 {Kd6 runs away from the checks, and it leaves your pawn to fend for itself.} 1... Kg7 2. f8=Q+ Kxf8 {The queen lives one move on f8 before his rook takes her straight back. Drawn.}) (1. Kc6 {Kc6 has the same problem as Kd6, just one square further off the pawn.} 1... Rf5 2. f8=Q Rxf8 {Drawn. His rook stops the new queen the moment she is born.}) (1. Kd4 {Kd4 goes backwards: the pawn cannot wait around for your king down there.} 1... Rf5 2. f8=Q Rxf8 {Drawn. Your king never reached the pawn, so his rook simply takes the new queen.}) (1. Kc4 {Kc4 is too far from everything that matters: neither the checks nor the pawn are in reach.} 1... Rf5 2. f8=Q Rxf8 {Drawn. His rook was always close enough to collect the new queen.}) 1... Ra6+ {He checks again. Keep walking your king straight at him. [%cal Ye6e7]} 2. Ke7 {Two steps up the ladder now, and your pawn on f7 is guarded at last. [%csl Ge7]} (2. Kf5 {Kf5 heads toward his rook, but it walks away from your own pawn.} 2... Kg7 3. f8=Q+ Kxf8 {Drawn. His king reaches f8 in time to take the new queen the moment she appears.}) (2. Kd7 {Kd7 goes sideways, and the pawn is left on its own again.} 2... Kg7 3. f8=Q+ Kxf8 {Drawn. Unescorted on f8, the new queen lasts only one single move.}) (2. Kd5 {Kd5 climbs back down the ladder, and all the work you did is undone.} 2... Rf6 3. f8=Q Rxf8 {Drawn. His rook blocks the pawn's road and then eats the new queen.}) (2. Ke5 {Ke5 steps backwards again, and the climb has to start all over.} 2... Kg7 3. f8=Q+ Kxf8 {Drawn. His king strolls over to f8 and simply takes the new queen there.}) 2... Ra7+ {Another check. Now step across, beside the pawn. [%cal Ye7f6]} 3. Kf6 {Kf6 stands beside the pawn, guarding it, and ready to build the bridge. [%csl Gf6]} (3. Kf8 {Ke7 also wins: you step out of the check instead of blocking it.} 3... Ra8+ {Still winning, just a slower road to the very same queen.}) (3. Ke6 {Ke6 also wins, though you have gone back a step from where you were.} 3... Ra6+ {Still winning, but here his rook comes to check you all over again.}) (3. Ke8 {Ke8 also wins, but your king keeps drifting away from the pawn.} 3... Ra8+ {Still winning, and still drifting further away from the pawn's own square.}) (3. Kd6 {Kd6 is now too far from the pawn on f7 to help it queen safely.} 3... Kg7 4. f8=Q+ Kxf8 {Drawn. His king gets to f8 first and takes the new queen straight away.}) (3. Kd8 {The same mistake as Kd6, and this time even further away.} 3... Kg7 4. f8=Q+ Kxf8 {Drawn. Your king is a whole column from the action, and his simply takes the new queen.}) 3... Ra6+ {The last check of the ladder. Block this one with the rook itself, on e6. [%cal Ye4e6]} 4. Re6 {The bridge! Your rook blocks the check, and the pawn walks straight home. Now watch it queen. [%csl Ge6][%cal Gf7f8][%tail]} (4. Ke7 {Ke7 also wins: you step out of the check instead of building the bridge.} 4... Ra7+ {Still winning, but building the bridge itself was the cleaner, faster way.}) (4. Kg5 {Running from the check with Kg5 leaves the pawn without any escort.} 4... Kg7 5. f8=Q+ Kxf8 {Drawn. Nobody is left to guard the new queen, and his king simply takes it.}) (4. Ke5 {Ke5 goes backwards, and the whole climb up the ladder is wasted.} 4... Kg7 5. f8=Q+ Kxf8 {Drawn. His king wanders back over to f8 and takes the new queen there.}) (4. Kf5 {The same retreat on f5, and it costs the very same result as Ke5 did.} 4... Kg7 5. f8=Q+ Kxf8 {Drawn. His king is right there on f5 to take the new queen the moment she appears.}) 4... Rxe6+ 5. Kxe6 5... Kg7 6. Ke7 6... Kg6 7. f8=Q {A new queen stands on f8, safely guarded. That is the bridge: your rook shielded the king from every check, and the pawn walked all the way home.} 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: check him out of the way"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "review"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/QezmK1sa"]
[FEN "8/2R5/1KPk4/8/8/8/7r/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/2R5/1KPk4/8/8/8/7r/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{Your pawn wants to run, but his king is standing right in its way. Move his king off that square.}
1. Rd7+ {Rd7+ shoves his king right off the pawn's own column. Now watch the pawn walk home. [%csl Gd7][%cal Gc6c8][%tail]} (1. Kb7 {Walking the king out with Kb7 starts the checks immediately, and this time they hold you off for good.} 1... Rb2+ 2. Kc8 Ra2 {He holds you off with checks that never stop coming. Drawn.}) (1. Rc8 {Rc8 puts the rook in front of its own pawn and falls asleep there.} 1... Rb2+ 2. Ka5 Rb1 {Drawn. His rook checks you from behind forever, while your own rook blocks its own pawn's road.}) (1. Re7 {Re7 steps the rook right next to his king, where it simply gets taken.} 1... Kxe7 {It is simply eaten by his king on e7. You are lost.}) (1. Ra7 {The same trouble happens on the other side of the board with Ra7.} 1... Rb2+ 2. Ka5 Kc5 {Lost. His king walks up beside the pawn, and your rook never even got behind it.}) (1. Kb5 {Walking the king away with Kb5 leaves the rook on c7 hanging.} 1... Kxc7 {The rook on c7 goes for free to his king. You are lost.}) 1... Ke5 2. c7 2... Kf5 3. c8=Q {A new queen stands on c8. One check was all it took to clear the whole road home.} 1-0


[Event "Rook endgames: block the last check"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/GhFJctMz/v1XtduA4"]
[FEN "8/1k2P3/8/2K4r/3R4/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/1k2P3/8/2K4r/3R4/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{His rook is checking you, and your pawn needs one more move to queen. Four legal moves here. Pick the right one.}
1. Rd5 {Rd5 steps right into the check and offers a trade he cannot refuse. [%csl Gd5]} (1. Kd6 {Kd6 also wins: the king walks straight out of the check, toward the pawn.} 1... Rh6+ {The checks go on for a while, but the win is never really in doubt.}) (1. Kb4 {Kb4 runs from the check and abandons the pawn completely.} 1... Re5 2. e8=Q Rxe8 {Drawn. Unescorted on e8, the new queen falls to his rook at once.}) (1. Kc4 {The same mistake as Kb4, just one square across the board on c4.} 1... Re5 2. e8=Q Rxe8 {Drawn. Undefended on e8, the new queen lasts only one move too.}) 1... Rxd5+ {He takes on d5 with check. Take his rook straight back with your king.} 2. Kxd5 {And now nothing on the board can stop the pawn. Now watch it queen. [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge7e8][%tail]} 2... Kc7 3. e8=Q {A new queen stands on e8, with his king still two squares short of reaching it.} 1-0
